</script><div class="ak-renderer-document"><div class="ak-renderer-sticky-safe-breakout-wrapper ak-renderer-flex-center-wrapper css-l5clsc"><div class="ak-renderer-sticky-safe-breakout-inner fabric-editor-breakout-mark fabric-editor-block-mark css-ozd7xs" data-mode="full-width" data-has-width="true" data-width="1800" style="width:min(1800px, var(--ak-editor--breakout-container-without-gutter-width))"><div class="layout-section-container" data-local-id="e63fb83303ce"><div data-layout-section="true" data-layout-columns="2"><div data-layout-column="true" data-column-width="66.66" style="flex-basis:66.66%" class="css-12lwizq"><div class="css-pw7jst"><div style="display:block;width:100%;position:absolute"></div><div data-layout-column-start="true" class="css-1rttiep"></div><p data-renderer-start-pos="3" data-local-id="8961be0c6d29">Protocols are an important first step in any systematic and rigorously conducted evidence synthesis, and this is no different for scoping reviews (Peters et al. 2022). JBI considers it a vital step prior to the conduct of a scoping review to ensure that it is well-planned, transparent and rigorous. It is also a requirement when following the JBI scoping review methodology to have an <em data-renderer-mark="true">a priori</em> protocol. However, the need for a protocol does vary among journals. One benefit of preparing a scoping review protocol is that it reduces <em data-renderer-mark="true">ad hoc</em> decision-making that can introduce bias within the review and impact its methodological quality. Another benefit of a protocol can be seen in its role as a document where all reviewers have agreed upon research aims and methodology. A protocol can be viewed as a recipe card, and if instructions are followed, it can improve your chances of a well-developed and successful review undertaking. Note that although we suggest that changes made from the development of the protocol to the review be avoided, sometimes this is not possible in a scoping review due to its iterative nature. If changes are made, deviations from the protocol with supporting justification should be made transparent within the final scoping review (Peters et al. 2022). This is discussed further in this chapter in the reporting section.<br/><br/></p><p data-renderer-start-pos="1359" data-local-id="5639be3037af"><strong data-renderer-mark="true">Should I register my scoping review?</strong></p><p data-renderer-start-pos="1397" data-local-id="470b13da959e">Registration of a systematic review is commonplace. Registration of systematic reviews can ‘promote transparency, help reduce potential for bias and serve to avoid unintended duplication of reviews’ (Stewart et al. 2012, p. 1), and it is supported by PROSPERO, a protocol registration database. Currently, PROSPERO does not accept scoping reviews to be registered (with that said, some scoping reviews do seem to have slipped through). However, there are other platforms where you can place your protocol, which are publicly listed to ensure transparency. These include <a title="https://osf.io/" data-renderer-mark="true" href="https://osf.io/" data-is-router-link="false" class="_2hGPVj98eZ _2i3C1kYbGa _2kH23qT6kp _1ubuxonXvL _34b3D2vc3e _1NDpvb6wH8 _2wtOC9vc3e _3e11vU6wH8 _0hSAgbHZdu _00uqCY98eZ _3xtjKN0Rl6 _0H9qDF6wH8 _24h04i2Njc _1mT5xdRAKS _0bjKvDcjp5 _0WKd3G4z3T _23rrnHo10F _1UtDYzM7Tp _32C2rk98eZ _0i9AWA0Rl6 _0mm6KQ6wH8 _10n1R5M7Tp _0DCa9DjOvk _3932vv0Rl6 _02cRtH6wH8 _1B4rbIo10F _0clgaMM7Tp _3WzCGijOvk _0NqVRt0Rl6 _3BKYwu6wH8 _3sFUREDdj4 _0CMRbkAwSC _2nTuUYWfPD _3ADzdEWfPD _1warJ9WfPD _4scvAwWyeI" data-testid="link-with-safety">OSF, </a>which provides standardised templates for reviews, and <a title="https://figshare.com/" data-renderer-mark="true" href="https://figshare.com/" data-is-router-link="false" class="_2hGPVj98eZ _2i3C1kYbGa _2kH23qT6kp _1ubuxonXvL _34b3D2vc3e _1NDpvb6wH8 _2wtOC9vc3e _3e11vU6wH8 _0hSAgbHZdu _00uqCY98eZ _3xtjKN0Rl6 _0H9qDF6wH8 _24h04i2Njc _1mT5xdRAKS _0bjKvDcjp5 _0WKd3G4z3T _23rrnHo10F _1UtDYzM7Tp _32C2rk98eZ _0i9AWA0Rl6 _0mm6KQ6wH8 _10n1R5M7Tp _0DCa9DjOvk _3932vv0Rl6 _02cRtH6wH8 _1B4rbIo10F _0clgaMM7Tp _3WzCGijOvk _0NqVRt0Rl6 _3BKYwu6wH8 _3sFUREDdj4 _0CMRbkAwSC _2nTuUYWfPD _3ADzdEWfPD _1warJ9WfPD _4scvAwWyeI" data-testid="link-with-safety">FigShare</a>.<br/><br/></p><p data-renderer-start-pos="2039" data-local-id="a1ff2d6079c6"><strong data-renderer-mark="true">Should I publish my protocol in a peer-reviewed journal?</strong></p><p data-renderer-start-pos="2097" data-local-id="5d9b6a08de92">Publishing your protocol in a peer-reviewed journal is not a requirement. Benefits of a published protocol could be increased visibility of the work, which would then hopefully increase transparency and reduce bias and duplication. A published protocol can also be used to request feedback on the scoping review methods. However, publication of a protocol can be expensive, many journals do not accept protocols and incorporating reviewer feedback within your work before starting your scoping review can add substantial time.</p><p data-renderer-start-pos="2625" data-local-id="880c16522b28">One other option is to ‘self-publish’ a protocol or have your protocol hosted on an institutional webpage or other location, such as ResearchGate. Some organisations, such as a university library or research outputs repository, can also help you assign the protocol a unique digital object identifier (DOI) for ease of citation. Such publications would generally not be considered peer-reviewed (unless this was undertaken through an appropriate and unbiased process, such as by the funding or commissioning body themselves), but they do still allow others to access and read the protocol, which enhances transparency.</p></div></div><div data-layout-column="true" data-column-width="33.33" style="flex-basis:33.33%" class="css-12lwizq"><div class="css-pw7jst"><div style="display:block;width:100%;position:absolute"></div><div data-layout-column-start="true" class="css-1rttiep"></div><p data-renderer-start-pos="3247" data-local-id="57bc593c2c0f"><br/><br/></p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><!-- data-loadable-begin="0Ufwq:EfLS5:z8NN7:qz-Pe:OP-5u" --><!-- data-loadable-end="0Ufwq:EfLS5:z8NN7:qz-Pe:OP-5u" --></div></div></div></div></div><div data-vc="eop-recommendations-wrapper"><!-- data-loadable-begin="11WpM:z8NN7:qz-Pe:OP-5u" --><!-- data-loadable-end="11WpM:z8NN7:qz-Pe:OP-5u" --></div><div data-vc="object-sidebar-wrapper" class="_08KCXeYbGa"><!-- data-loadable-begin="liSnz:z8NN7:qz-Pe:OP-5u" --><div data-block-controls-suppression="true" data-testid="object-sidebar-container" data-vc="object-sidebar-container" style="right:calc(var(--rightPanelWidth, 0px));margin-right:0" class="_23rrnH9JLe _1mT5xdy1CL _0Bm75GdnbC _42Yu6BiZR3 _1pp6NdYbGa _27asfwIZbP _1ZnuxbNHnM _1wydGW2ADO" data-ssr-placeholder="liSnz:z8NN7:qz-Pe:OP-5u-0"><div><div class="_39xV02p0Oa _2XsHFMIZbP"><div role="presentation"><div style="padding-bottom:var(--ds-space-100, 8px);visibility:visible;opacity:0" data-testid="collapsible-hover-toggle" class="_313842IZbP _0adFHsIZbP _1DCdHiIZbP _2XsHFMIZbP _39xV02Va5s"><button aria-live="polite" type="button" class="_2hGPVj98eZ _2i3C1kYbGa _2kH23qT6kp _1ubuxonXvL _34b3D2vc3e _1NDpvb6wH8 _2wtOC9vc3e _3e11vU6wH8 _0hSAgbHZdu _30huDKYbGa _24h04i2Njc _034jHtYbGa _0wOv4Q7zJH _0bjKvDS5Zs _2sNsIgmnOE _23rrnHyKLB _2V7sgOdnbC _4c065UgVvo _3iDTPbccYA _39xV02Z49V _1EqgXlN91d _1mT5xdRAKS _0hPCNLQame _37wbyuDIqT _17lj8pgVvo _3YEZNOdnbC _0jFU0VZ49V _1kk5Tkf7mX _03SyCPPgdg _0LQT5EPgdg _2fwxA5DIqT _1RrJLit2nV _2Gfj6iOLYo _0EnhYgPgdg _4lwhQT6wH8 _0dpm39NvrM _2tbAUcYbGa _2WYcYGWVPG _3sFUREiSCN _0KLXru66of _1UtDYzZpcK _2nTuUYZpcK _10n1R5ZpcK _0clgaMZpcK _0CMRbkZpcK _1eOmH6o126 _3gzVzLN4oS _00uqCY98eZ _3xtjKNYbGa _0H9qDF6wH8 _32C2rk98eZ _0i9AWAYbGa _0mm6KQ6wH8 _0DCa9D98eZ _3932vvYbGa _02cRtH6wH8 _3WzCGi98eZ _0NqVRtYbGa _3BKYwu6wH8" data-testid="collapsible-hover-button"><span class="_23rrnHBXJV _3iDTPbQ4SZ _2O5Vijdn